After initial checkout:
  (1) run setup.sh to setup the git-disk repo
  (2) edit psqldb-env.sh to suit your environment

Then run
  (1) init.sh to create the tmp git repo
  (2) init-psqldb.sh to set up a local psqldb data in another tmp directory

As both these scripts create a tmpfs mount there will need to rerun after a
reboot or the mounts are removed.

Once everything is set up finally run
  update.sh to update the diff branch with the latest commits

The tmpfs mounts will continue to take up memory.  They can be safely removed
(unmounted).  However if a PostgreSQL server is still running it may need to
be stopped first.  The easiest way is to stop the server is to source the
psqldb-env.sh script and then use the pgctl alias to stop the server.  For example:
  . psqldb-env.sh
  pgctl stop

To rerun the scripts on existing commits the easiest thing to do is to reset
the diff branch in the git-disk directory.  For example:

  cd git-disk
  git branch -f diff 06b9c75aa72a171dc18a82c5c4a18d092ce8e3e5

and rerun update.sh.

The creation of the tmpfs mounts is for both speed and to reduce SSD wear.
The mounts are not strictly needed and the scripts can easily be hacked to avoid
requiring them.




